      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Framing the issue as \"fundamentally flawed\" depends heavily on the economic philosophy one adopts, as growth and equity are often competing goals. From a strict pure production standpoint, a reduction in labor supply could be seen as detrimental to immediate growth. However, proponents argue that a healthier, more secure population leads to better human capital and long-term societal productivity. The debate really lies in whether the benefits of social stability outweigh the potential short-term drag on the labor force.",
      "source": "target",
